\details{
\strong{Method}

With \eqn{G = -\frac{1}{2}JAJ} as in \code{\link[=pcoa]{pcoa()}} and \code{\link[=dbrda]{dbrda()}}, and its
eigen-decomposition \eqn{G = V\Lambda V^T}, the principal coordinates with
positive eigenvalues are \eqn{Y = V_+\Lambda_+^{1/2}}, and
\eqn{G_+ = YY^T} is the positive part of \eqn{G}. \code{capscale()} is the
redundancy analysis of \eqn{Y} on the constraints, which is \code{\link[=dbrda]{dbrda()}} with
\eqn{G_+} in place of \eqn{G}. As in \code{vegan}, eigenvalues within
\code{max(e, e * lambda1)} of 0, for \eqn{e} = \code{sqrt(.Machine$double.eps)} and
\eqn{\lambda_1} the largest, count as 0.

For Euclidean distances (such as Euclidean, Hellinger, or Aitchison
distances), \eqn{G} has no negative eigenvalues, so \eqn{G_+ = G}.
\code{capscale()} first looks for a negative eigenvalue, by block Lanczos
iteration on \eqn{-G} with products straight from \code{dm} (as \code{\link[=pcoa]{pcoa()}}
finds the largest eigenvalues). It stops as soon as one is certain, and
otherwise runs until the smallest eigenvalue of \eqn{G} has converged.
Having found none, the rest is exactly \code{\link[=dbrda]{dbrda()}}'s, in as little memory.
That is quick when \eqn{G} has many eigenvalues at 0, such as for
Euclidean distances between samples with fewer features than samples.
When the smallest eigenvalues are close together, convergence can take
many steps; after about \eqn{n/10} of them (each one pass over \code{dm}), when
going on would cost about as much as the full eigen-decomposition,
\code{capscale()} stops looking and decomposes \eqn{G}, as below, which gives
the same results either way.

Otherwise, \eqn{G_+} needs every eigenvalue of \eqn{G}, as in
\code{vegan::capscale()}: \code{capscale()} forms \eqn{G} (\eqn{8n^2} bytes, such as
200 MB for 5000 samples), reduces it to tridiagonal form, finds all of its
eigenvalues, and then the eigenvectors of the positive ones, or of the
others if they are fewer, by inverse iteration. That takes time
proportional to \eqn{n^3}, though several times less than a full
eigen-decomposition: for 2000 Bray-Curtis distances, about 2 seconds on
one core, against 12 for \code{vegan::capscale()}. The reduction and the
eigenvectors are computed in parallel on \code{cpus} threads.

\strong{capscale() or dbrda()?}

See \code{\link[=dbrda]{dbrda()}}. In short: \code{dbrda()} analyzes the distances as they are,
and quickly for any number of samples; \code{capscale()} analyzes the closest
Euclidean configuration, so its eigenvalues are never negative, but it
ignores the rest and takes much longer for large non-Euclidean data.
Another way to avoid negative eigenvalues is to make the distances
Euclidean first, such as by the square roots of Bray-Curtis distances
(Legendre & Legendre 2012, Table 7.2), or with \code{\link[=pcoa]{pcoa()}}'s \code{correction},
and then to use \code{dbrda()}.

\strong{Matching vegan}

\code{capscale()} gives the same scores, eigenvalues, inertia (including the
imaginary inertia), \eqn{R^2}, biplot scores and centroids as
\code{vegan::capscale()} given distances, up to the signs of the axes. As
there, distances are divided by \eqn{\sqrt{n-1}} when any is 4 or more
(see "Scale" in \code{\link[=dbrda]{dbrda()}}). \code{vegan::capscale()} can also take a community
table, add a constant to the distances (\code{add}), or take their square roots
(\code{sqrt.dist}), and gives species scores; \code{capscale()} does not.

\code{capscale()} is named after \code{vegan::capscale()}. Anderson & Willis's (2003)
"canonical analysis of principal coordinates" (CAP) is a related but
different method.

\strong{Axis signs and reproducibility}

As for \code{\link[=dbrda]{dbrda()}}: each axis's largest score in absolute value is
positive, and results are identical from run to run and for any number
of \code{cpus}.
}
\section{Ordination result}{

Every ordination function (\code{\link[=pcoa]{pcoa()}}, \code{\link[=pca]{pca()}}, \code{\link[=rpca]{rpca()}}, \code{\link[=dbrda]{dbrda()}},
\code{\link[=capscale]{capscale()}}, \code{\link[=mds]{mds()}}, \code{\link[=nmds]{nmds()}}, \code{\link[=isomap]{isomap()}}, \code{\link[=tsne]{tsne()}}, \code{\link[=umap]{umap()}}, and so
\code{\link[=dist_ord]{dist_ord()}}) returns the same kind of
object: a numeric matrix with one row per sample and one column per
axis, so that it can go straight into a data frame. Rows are
named after the samples, when the input has names. Columns are named
after the method: \code{"PCoA1"}, \code{"PC1"}, \code{"RPCA1"}, \code{"dbRDA1"} or \code{"CAP1"}
(then \code{"MDS1"} for unconstrained axes), \code{"MDS1"}, \code{"NMDS1"},
\code{"Isomap1"}, \code{"TSNE1"}, \code{"UMAP1"}, and so on.

Its attributes always begin with these three, in this order, each with
one value per column:
\itemize{
\item \code{method}: The method's ID, e.g. \code{"pcoa"} or \code{"nmds"}.
\item \code{eig}: Each axis's eigenvalue, on the method's own scale: \code{\link[=pcoa]{pcoa()}}
and \code{\link[=isomap]{isomap()}} give the eigenvalues of Gower's centered matrix (sums
of squares); \code{\link[=pca]{pca()}} and \code{\link[=rpca]{rpca()}} give variances, which are \code{\link[=pcoa]{pcoa()}}'s
divided by \eqn{n - 1} for \eqn{n} samples; and \code{\link[=dbrda]{dbrda()}} and
\code{\link[=capscale]{capscale()}} follow \code{vegan}, which also divides by \eqn{n - 1} when any
distance is 4 or more. \code{NA} for \code{\link[=mds]{mds()}}, \code{\link[=nmds]{nmds()}}, \code{\link[=tsne]{tsne()}}, and
\code{\link[=umap]{umap()}}, which have no eigenvalues.
\item \code{prop}: The proportion of the total variance (or inertia) that each
axis explains. Unlike \code{eig}, it is comparable between methods, so use
it to label axes. \code{NA} where it isn't defined.
}

Attributes particular to the method follow them, and last, the records
of how it was made (see \link{ecodive_steps}): \code{dist}, the distance
matrix's, for the methods that take distances, and \code{ord}, the
ordination's own (its method, display name and settings). The matrix has class
\code{"ecodive_ord"} (as well as \code{"matrix"}), so that \code{print()} lists the
other attributes by name rather than in full, and \code{plot()} draws the
samples on the first two axes, labelled from the attributes, with any
loadings, constraints and centroids on top (see \code{\link[=plot.ecodive_ord]{plot.ecodive_ord()}}).
\code{plot(ord, "Species", iris)} colours the samples by a column of
metadata. Subsetting with \code{[}, or \code{as.data.frame()}, gives a plain
matrix or data frame without the attributes.
}

